			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://mstdn.social/@mattwilcox/110497628460492483",
			"author": {
				"name": "Matt Wilcox",
				"url": "https://mstdn.social/@mattwilcox",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/287/949/593/182/322/original/ea34d8a24964f103.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-06-06T14:00:04.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> </p><p>All things that are central to radical and revolutionary events were once available only to the rich. It's how they came to exist at all. Writing, Books, Candles, Bulbs, Radios... each and every one \"radical\" and \"revolutionary\". Culture changing tech. And always were, even before they did.</p><p>Revolutionary *things* exist before the revolution. The things are still radical and revolutionary - the revolution itself just hasn't happened yet due to the lack of general accessibility.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
		{
			"id": "110497649312326726",
			"parentId": 0,
			"url": "https://a11y.info/@ben/110497649284919046",
			"author": {
				"name": "Ben Myers 🦖",
				"url": "https://a11y.info/@ben",
				"avatarUrl": "https://cdn.masto.host/fediversezachleatcom/cache/accounts/avatars/109/287/850/606/919/513/original/60b6c78eb03590ac.jpg"
			},
			"date": "2023-06-06T14:05:22.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> You see this a ton with disability dongles, or the kinds of hackathon-winning tech that's apparently designed to fix some aspect of disability but for which clearly few/no disabled people have been consulted (<a href=\"https://blog.castac.org/2022/04/disability-dongle/\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\"><span class=\"invisible\">https://</span><span class=\"ellipsis\">blog.castac.org/2022/04/disabi</span><span class=\"invisible\">lity-dongle/</span></a>)</p><p>Even if the tech *could* be useful, it's invariably sold in hyper-limited quantities at prohibitively expensive prices, thus ensuring it'll never change the world as much as its creators claim.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
