			"url": "https://weekly.org/@mlmiller/110504394862576235",
			"author": {
				"name": "mlmiller@weekly.org",
				"url": "https://weekly.org/@mlmiller",
				"avatarUrl": "https://fediverse.zachleat.com/avatars/original/missing.png"
			},
			"date": "2023-06-07T18:40:51.000Z",
			"content": "<p><span class=\"h-card\"><a href=\"https://fediverse.zachleat.com/@zachleat\" class=\"u-url mention\" rel=\"nofollow noopener\" target=\"_blank\">@<span>zachleat</span></a></span> I was. I was just saying we need to look past v1.0 of new technologies and ”invent a future” where costs come down and potential impact goes up for all. While the benefits have yet to be demonstrated, we should not dismiss the tech simply because the first one out the door is too expensive for mass adoption. So were computers in general, so was the Lisa, and so on. I am more worried to understand how such inventions may ultimately help us learn.</p>",
			"via": "fediverse"
		},
